---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";

const card = archiveAi.rewind;
const attempts = 1;

const network = [
  ["Zero successful requests", "Holds", "is-ok", "The restriction blocked the lookup, so nothing got through."],
  ["Zero attempted requests", "Fails", "is-bad", `The audit log shows ${attempts} denied attempt.`],
];

const diff = [
  { field: "species", old: "red maple", now: "Acer rubrum", why: "The saved lookup table maps the common name to the scientific name, and the configuration selects scientific names.", ok: true },
  { field: "inspection_date", old: "changed", now: "changed", why: "No saved input or rule accounts for it.", ok: false },
];

const checks = [
  "The report gives a hash for every file it read. What else does it need to establish whether any expected files were missing?",
  "The rebuild ran with network access blocked and logged one denied connection attempt. Which network claim can you make, and which claim fails?",
  "A tree’s inspection date differs from the old output, and the report cannot trace the change to a saved input or rule. What should the acceptance decision be, and what does the difference tell you?",
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("rewind")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's rebuild-criteria study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn="In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Rewind and name the line in a report that would show your own criterion was met."
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">{card.title}</h2>
  <p>Write acceptance criteria so someone else can decide whether the rebuild passed using the report and its evidence. For example, suppose you’re rebuilding a city’s street-tree inventory from saved survey files and a species lookup table.</p>

  <h2>1. Account for every expected input</h2>
  <p class="pa-criterion"><strong>Acceptance criterion.</strong> The report lists every saved file the rebuild read, with its relative path and the SHA-256 hash of the exact bytes used. It also checks those files against an expected-input manifest and lists every missing file, whether it was required, and how its absence was handled.</p>
  <p>For the tree inventory, the report would record hashes for <code>surveys/north.csv</code> and <code>species_lookup.csv</code>. If <code>surveys/south.csv</code> was required and missing, the rebuild fails acceptance. If an optional corrections file was missing, the report records that the rebuild proceeded without applying corrections.</p>

  <h2>2. Identify the code and effective configuration</h2>
  <p class="pa-criterion"><strong>Acceptance criterion.</strong> The report records the full Git commit, the rebuild command, and a fingerprint of the effective configuration after defaults and overrides are applied. The report includes the configuration itself or points to a saved copy so another person can rerun it. Require a clean checkout if the commit is meant to identify all the code used. Capture the runtime and dependency versions when they can affect the result.</p>
  <p>For the tree inventory, the configuration might specify how to parse survey dates, normalize species names, and sort rows. Hashing a consistently serialized copy of those effective settings lets a reviewer check that a rerun used the same rules.</p>

  <h2>3. Make the network claim verifiable</h2>
  <p class="pa-criterion"><strong>Acceptance criterion.</strong> Run the rebuild in an environment that blocks network access for the rebuild and its child processes. Save evidence that the restriction was active throughout the run. If the requirement also forbids <em>attempted</em> requests, capture an audit log that records denied attempts and require it to show zero.</p>
  <p>These are two different checks. A blocked lookup for an unfamiliar species is consistent with zero successful requests, but it fails a requirement of zero attempts. State which claim your evidence supports.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">One blocked species lookup, two claims</p>
    <div class="pr-scroll">
      <table class="pr-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Claim</th><th scope="col">Verdict</th><th scope="col">Evidence</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{network.map(([c, v, tone, e]) => <tr><th scope="row">{c}</th><td><span class:list={["pr-chip", tone]}>{v}</span></td><td>{e}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <h2>4. Explain every difference from the old output</h2>
  <p class="pa-criterion"><strong>Acceptance criterion.</strong> Record hashes for the old and rebuilt outputs. Compare rows by a stable key such as <code>tree_id</code>, and report every added or removed row and every changed field. For each changed value, show the old and new values and identify the saved input or transformation rule that explains the change. A single explanation can cover many changes if the report identifies every affected row and field.</p>
  <p>For example, tree <code>T-104</code> changes from <code>red maple</code> to <code>Acer rubrum</code> because the saved lookup table maps the common name to the scientific name and the rebuild configuration selects scientific names. If its <code>inspection_date</code> also changes and no saved input or rule accounts for it, acceptance fails. The unexplained change tells you the evidence is incomplete. Further investigation is needed to determine whether the cause is a bug, an untracked input or setting, nondeterminism, or a problem with the baseline.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">Diff for tree <code>T-104</code></p>
    <ul class="pa-diff">
      {diff.map((d) => (
        <li class={d.ok ? "is-ok" : "is-bad"}>
          <p><code>{d.field}</code>{d.ok ? <span><s>{d.old}</s> → <b>{d.now}</b></span> : <span>value changed</span>}<span class:list={["pr-chip", d.ok ? "is-ok" : "is-bad"]}>{d.ok ? "Explained" : "Unexplained"}</span></p>
          <p>{d.why}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <figcaption>One unexplained change is enough to fail the rebuild, however plausible the new value looks.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>Riley's criteria in Rewind ask for the reply's four things: a capture manifest that identifies each saved schedule by content hash and lists Dunmore as unavailable, the parser fingerprint beside the build revision, zero network requests, and a diff of Tuesday's table against the rebuild that explains every changed cell.</p>
    <p>For the network claim, Kick's lab takes the reply's stricter path. It counts every connection attempt, and it first shows the counter catching a test request, so its zero is evidence rather than silence.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map((q) => <li><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  :global(.pr) .pa-criterion { padding: 0.6rem 0.85rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--tl-derived-edge); border-radius: 6px; background: var(--tl-card); }
  .pa-diff { display: grid; gap: 0.45rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .pa-diff li { padding: 0.55rem 0.8rem; border: 1px solid var(--pr-green); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-diff li.is-bad { border: 1.5px dashed var(--pr-red); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-diff p { margin: 0 0 0.2rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-diff p:first-child { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 0.3rem 0.7rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-diff p:last-child { margin: 0; color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-diff s { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-diff b { color: var(--sl-color-white); }
  .pa-diff .pr-chip, .pa-diff :global(.pr-chip) { margin-left: auto; }
</style>
